**Fix figures 4 and 5. Ship the other five.** Figure 4 makes different breach events look directly comparable. Figure 5 leaves the forecast’s starting checkpoint implicit.

Coordinates below are approximate positions in the supplied 2× PNGs, measured from the upper-left corner. Readability judgments are for an 850 px display.

## 1. `edit-effect-scatter` — Ship

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** Text is clear. The blue acceptance line deliberately crosses both dashed zero guides at the origin. The region labels make the accepted side clear. There are no arrows.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** The legend and the two zero ticks are readable at roughly 10–11 px. The repeated “accepted, tail harmed” label earns its place by identifying both the orange points and the shaded region.
- **(f) Five-second read.** Yes. The mean rule accepts edits that harm the tail. The orange points below zero and to the right of the boundary show it immediately.
- **(g) Fix.** None needed. The shaded region and its direct label carry the result even if the reader skips the legend.

## 2. `drift-sign-map` — Ship

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** The text clears the curves and guides. The three intersections on the π = 0.125 guide are intentional and marked with circles. There are no arrows.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** The ticks are the smallest text at roughly 10–11 px. Keep the instruction to read each curve on its own. In the space between curves, whether selection helps or harms depends on which spread ratio applies.
- **(f) Five-second read.** Yes. The correlation threshold changes with tail weight and relative spread. The highlighted −0.143 value is easy to locate.
- **(g) Fix.** None needed. Keep the −0.143 callout beside the solid-curve marker at about (605, 706) and the three line styles in the legend.

## 3. `timing-regimes-geometry` — Ship

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** The seed labels clear the circles and paths. All three arrowheads point toward the mean optimum, including seed 1’s up-left arrow around (1370, 762). The paths meet at that optimum as intended. Seed 2’s diamond marks the boundary crossing, with a matching diamond at the inset peak.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** The labels and inset headings are readable at 850 px. Keep the sentence above the ball. It explains why entering the ball changes the direction of cumulative gain.
- **(f) Five-second read.** Yes for the three outcomes. A reader can see gain, rise then decline, and decline. Linking the inverted-U peak to the boundary crossing takes a closer look.
- **(g) Fix.** None required. A small `0` at the left end of each inset’s dashed baseline would make the starting reference explicit.

## 4. `guarantee-validity-and-price` — Fix

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** The green markers clear each other, including at n = 10. The certification square at about (2252, 635) sits to the right of the terminal tick and axis endpoint at x ≈ 2240, so the dodge can look like a sample size above 20. No arrows or lines cross text.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** The event definitions around (350, 1050) are physically readable at roughly 10 px, but their placement makes them easy to skip. They are essential to interpreting the left panel and belong beside the method labels. The exact simulation values can stay in the caption.
- **(f) Five-second read.** Partly. The green methods fall below the nominal breach rate and retain less majority gain than the oracle. The quick read misses that checkpoint certification counts breaches at the deployed checkpoint, while the other methods count a breach at any accepted parent during the run. “Per step vs parent” makes that first orange row especially easy to misread as a per-step breach rate.
- **(g) Fix.** Retitle the left panel “How often is the tail margin breached?” Rename the first row “Parent-relative gate.” Put the event definitions beside the relevant rows, and extend the right x-axis past x ≈ 2265 while keeping the 20 tick at x ≈ 2240.

## 5. `instrumented-loop` — Fix

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** The arrows clear the text. The feedback arrow clearly returns to Parent harness, the checkpoint arrow lands at Sealed test people, and both bottom arrows point into Compare. The ambiguity is in the forecast box. “Generation-50 tail change” leaves the starting checkpoint implicit, while the sealed test box explicitly says generations 0 to 50.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** The labels are readable at about 10–12 px. Keep the generation-10 freeze in the forecast box. It establishes when the prediction was committed.
- **(f) Five-second read.** The editing loop reads quickly. The experiment’s main point takes longer. A forecast frozen at generation 10 is checked against the same generation-0-to-50 change measured on sealed test people.
- **(g) Fix.** In the Forecast box at about (2080, 1060), use “Predict tail change from generation 0 to 50” and “Freeze at generation 10.” Rename “saved checkpoints” at about (355, 800) to “Generation 0 and 50 checkpoints.”

## 6. `stable-person-splits` — Ship

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** The cell labels, headers, and legend clear each other. There are no connectors or arrows to trace.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** The legend body is the smallest text at roughly 10 px and remains readable. The guard description wraps “loop” onto its own line. Keep the per-fold counts and short role descriptions in the figure.
- **(f) Five-second read.** Yes. Each outer fold uses one block for sealed testing, one for guard scoring, and three for development. The grid makes the rotation apparent.
- **(g) Fix.** None required. If tightening the legend near (1928, 540), use “About 34 per fold. Scores every candidate. Scores stay hidden from that fold’s loop.”

## 7. `dose-response-design` — Ship

- **(a–c) Collisions, crossings, direction.** Cell text fits. The blue arrow clears the grid and callout and points down from 16 to 256 tail items. The bracket joins the π = 0.20 and π = 0.40 column centers and marks the interval where the sign changes.
- **(d–e) Size and caption.** Cell values and headers are readable. The units note is small but legible at roughly 10 px. The callout repeats values in the last column, but it helps readers see what changes as the number of items increases.
- **(f) Five-second read.** Yes. Increasing tail weight changes predicted harm to gain. Increasing the number of tail items leaves the sign unchanged at each displayed weight.
- **(g) Fix.** None needed. Keep the bracket spanning the two tested weights and the “near π = 0.23” wording, which identifies the model’s predicted crossing between them.
